# =============================================================================
# StreamHub — self-hosted LiveKit media server (OSS quick-install)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values, OR just run ./install.sh
# which generates a `.env` with strong random secrets for you.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then edit
#   docker compose up -d
#
# NEVER commit the real `.env`. All *_SECRET / *_PASS / *_TOKEN values below
# must be long and random in production.
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public identity / TLS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single public domain that fronts everything (Caddy reverse proxy + TLS).
#   Production: a real hostname with a DNS A record pointing at this server's
#               public IP (DNS-only, NOT proxied through Cloudflare — WebRTC
#               media is UDP and won't traverse an HTTP proxy).
#   Local test: `localhost` (Caddy serves a local self-signed cert on :443).
STREAMHUB_DOMAIN=streamhub.example.com

# Email used for Let's Encrypt / ACME registration (real domains only).
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LiveKit — SFU keys (shared by server + ingress + egress)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate with `livekit-server generate-keys`, or install.sh makes them.
# The key name conventionally starts with "API".
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=APIchangeme000000
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=change-me-a-long-random-secret

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# StreamHub core (NestJS) — runtime config
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local bind of the core HTTP service (the reverse proxy targets this).
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3020

# How the core reaches LiveKit (same host — see docker-compose host networking).
LIVEKIT_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:7880

# The ws/wss URL and RTMP host handed to browser clients & publishers in join
# links / tokens. On a real domain these ride the single TLS vhost via `/rtc`.
#   Real domain: PUBLIC_WS_URL=wss://streamhub.example.com  RTMP_PUBLIC_HOST=streamhub.example.com
#   Localhost:   PUBLIC_WS_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:7880          RTMP_PUBLIC_HOST=127.0.0.1
PUBLIC_WS_URL=wss://streamhub.example.com
RTMP_PUBLIC_HOST=streamhub.example.com

# HLS edge/CDN caching — OFF by default. When on, live HLS *playlists* (.m3u8)
# become briefly cacheable (short live TTL + stale-while-revalidate; longer once
# finished) so an edge/CDN reverse proxy can collapse concurrent viewers and
# offload fan-out bandwidth. `.ts` segments are ALWAYS immutable + long-cached.
# Safe to leave on even without a CDN. Turn on when fronting /hls with a cache
# (deploy/nginx-hls-edge.conf or deploy/Caddyfile.edge; see features/HLS-CDN.md).
#STREAMHUB_HLS_CDN=on

# RTMPS ingest (TLS-terminated RTMP-in) — OFF by default. When on, the ingress
# endpoints surface an `rtmps_url` next to `rtmp_url`. Turning it on is two steps:
#   1) STREAMHUB_RTMPS_ENABLE=true here (so core advertises the rtmps:// URL), and
#   2) start the stunnel front:  docker compose --profile rtmps up -d
# The front TLS-terminates :STREAMHUB_RTMPS_PORT and forwards to the plaintext
# ingress on :1935, reusing the domain cert (see STREAMHUB_RTMPS_CERT_DIR below).
#STREAMHUB_RTMPS_ENABLE=true
#STREAMHUB_RTMPS_PORT=1936
# Public host for the rtmps:// URL. Empty → reuse RTMP_PUBLIC_HOST.
#RTMPS_PUBLIC_HOST=
# Where the stunnel front reads the cert. It expects fullchain.pem + privkey.pem.
#   nginx+certbot: /etc/letsencrypt/live/<your-domain>   (used as-is, zero copy)
#   otherwise:     drop the two PEMs into ./data/certs/rtmps (the default)
#STREAMHUB_RTMPS_CERT_DIR=/etc/letsencrypt/live/streamhub.example.com

# TURN relay (embedded LiveKit TURN/TLS) — OFF by default. Without it, clients
# behind symmetric NAT / VPN / corporate firewalls (Safari/iOS strictest) can't
# connect; LiveKit hands ICE servers to clients automatically (browser untouched).
# v1: ORIGIN + nginx shape only (needs on-disk letsencrypt PEMs). Easiest path is
# the installer: `--turn [--turn-domain turn.<domain>]` (issues the cert, opens
# 5349/tcp+3478/udp, flips ENABLE=true once the cert exists). To wire by hand:
#   1) get a cert for turn.<domain>  (its own DNS A record → this host)
#   2) STREAMHUB_TURN_DOMAIN + STREAMHUB_TURN_CERT_DIR=/etc/letsencrypt below, then
#   3) STREAMHUB_TURN_ENABLE=true and `streamhub restart` (livekit loads cert at boot)
#STREAMHUB_TURN_ENABLE=true
#STREAMHUB_TURN_DOMAIN=turn.streamhub.example.com
#STREAMHUB_TURN_CERT_DIR=/etc/letsencrypt

# RTMP→HLS passthrough (remux, no transcode — AntMedia-style) — OFF by default.
# When on, an embedded RTMP front accepts an H.264/AAC push and REMUXES it
# straight to HLS (`ffmpeg -c copy`), BYPASSING LiveKit → near-zero CPU, no GPU.
# It runs on its OWN port (default 1937), fully independent of LiveKit's :1935
# ingress and the transcode/WebRTC/egress path. Per-app opt-in via
# `rtmp.passthrough` (or the `rtmp-passthrough` preset) and the ingress API
# `{mode:'passthrough'}`. Mutually exclusive with transcoding/adaptive. See
# features/rtmp-passthrough.md.
#STREAMHUB_RTMP_PASSTHROUGH_ENABLE=on
#STREAMHUB_RTMP_PASSTHROUGH_PORT=1937

# Single-port :1935 RTMP front via MediaMTX — OFF by default. When on, ONE public
# port (:1935) serves BOTH planes: MediaMTX terminates every publish and fires
# loopback hooks to the core, which classifies by stream key — a `ptk_` key →
# passthrough remux, else (a real `live/<key>`) → forward to the LiveKit ingress
# (moved to the internal :11935). Replaces the multi-port model (LiveKit :1935 +
# passthrough :1937+), which stays the fallback when this is off. Enable it as a
# set (the installer's --mediamtx does all of it + installs the binary/systemd
# unit; for plain compose also run `--profile mediamtx`):
#   STREAMHUB_MEDIAMTX_ENABLE=true              # core builds passthrough URLs on :1935 + validates the hooks
#   STREAMHUB_LIVEKIT_INGRESS_RTMP_PORT=11935   # LiveKit ingress vacates :1935; also the core's forward target
#   STREAMHUB_MEDIAMTX_HOOK_SECRET=<random>     # shared secret for the /internal/mediamtx/* hooks (long, random)
# See features/rtmp-single-port.md.
#STREAMHUB_MEDIAMTX_ENABLE=true
#STREAMHUB_LIVEKIT_INGRESS_RTMP_PORT=11935
#STREAMHUB_MEDIAMTX_HOOK_SECRET=
# Public RTMP port MediaMTX owns (also the port core advertises in passthrough
# push URLs). Default 1935 — rarely changed.
#STREAMHUB_MEDIAMTX_RTMP_PORT=1935
# MediaMTX control-API base the core queries on boot re-arm (GET /v3/paths/list)
# to restore live remuxes/forwards after a core restart. Must match the front's
# loopback apiAddress. Default fits deploy/mediamtx.yml — rarely changed.
#STREAMHUB_MEDIAMTX_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9997

# Ingest ENCODING profile for the LiveKit-transcoded ingress (global switch).
#  - livekit  (DEFAULT — stable): StreamHub attaches NO encoding opinions;
#    LiveKit decides everything (its default = 3-layer 720p simulcast, ~3
#    encodes per stream). The long-standing legacy behaviour.
#  - efficient (opt-in, experimental): StreamHub attaches an explicit
#    SINGLE-layer preset (or a capped 3-layer preset when the app enables
#    adaptive) so the CPU-heavy simulcast default can never leak through.
#    Measured on prod: 3×1080p ≈ 100% of 8 cores → ~24%.
# Server-wide; on a cluster set it per node. Read at boot → restart to apply.
# Flipping back to `livekit` is the instant rollback. Applies to NEW ingresses
# (delete+recreate an existing one to re-encode it). features/efficient-transcode.md
#STREAMHUB_INGEST_PROFILE=livekit

# Redis (used by LiveKit ingress/egress coordination AND BullMQ job queues).
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secret that signs the UI login JWT (POST /api/v1/auth/login).
STREAMHUB_JWT_SECRET=change-me-a-long-random-jwt-secret

# Break-glass admin login for the dashboard. Login is disabled if either is
# empty. On success the API returns a JWT signed with STREAMHUB_JWT_SECRET.
ADMIN_USER=admin
ADMIN_PASS=change-me-a-strong-admin-password

# Global API bearer token (sk_...) seeded into the DB by install.sh. This is the
# credential you pass as `Authorization: Bearer sk_...` to the REST API. It is
# stored here only so re-running install.sh stays idempotent; the DB keeps a hash.
STREAMHUB_API_TOKEN=sk_change_me

# Authorization + quota enforcement rollout: off | log | on.
# Fase-0 security: the CODE default (unset) is now `on` (secure-by-default), and
# fresh installs should keep `on`: RBAC is enforced, per-app `sk_` tokens are
# confined to their own app, and over-quota requests are rejected. `log` runs the
# same checks but only LOGS what they would block (use it to migrate an existing
# deployment that ran unenforced). GLOBAL `sk_` tokens and the break-glass admin
# always bypass RBAC — they are never locked out.
STREAMHUB_AUTHZ_ENFORCE=on

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage / data
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Path INSIDE the containers where the core (and egress) read/write data:
# data/streamhub.db, apps/<name>/{recordings,snapshots,hls,samples}, logs/, sdk/.
# Must be identical for `core` and `egress` (egress writes MP4s where core looks).
DATA_DIR=/data

# Path ON THE HOST that is bind-mounted to DATA_DIR in both containers.
STREAMHUB_HOST_DATA_DIR=./data

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email (SMTP) — magic-link / invites (optional but recommended)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#STREAMHUB_SUPERADMIN_EMAIL=info@example.com

# Public user registration / self-signup ("Crear cuenta" in the dashboard +
# POST /auth/signup). UNSET/0 = invite-only (default): new emails get 403;
# invited pending users can still complete their signup. 1/true/on/yes =
# anyone can create an account + their own free-plan tenant. Surfaced to the
# SPA via GET /auth/config so it hides "Create account" when off.
# STREAMHUB_ALLOW_SIGNUP is a backward-compatible alias; the canonical flag
# wins when both are set.
#STREAMHUB_PUBLIC_REGISTRATION=0
#STREAMHUB_ALLOW_SIGNUP=0
#STREAMHUB_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.com
#STREAMHUB_SMTP_PORT=587
#STREAMHUB_SMTP_USER=no-reply@example.com
#STREAMHUB_SMTP_PASS=change-me
#STREAMHUB_SMTP_FROM=StreamHub <no-reply@example.com>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability / cluster
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Token that guards GET /metrics (Prometheus) on the core. Fase-0 default-deny:
# UNSET = /metrics returns 404 (disabled, never leaks). Set a token to enable
# scraping with `Authorization: Bearer <token>` (or `?token=`). The nginx/Caddy
# vhost ALSO denies /metrics from the outside — scrape locally at
# 127.0.0.1:3020/metrics.
#METRICS_TOKEN=mtk_change_me

# LiveKit's own services expose native Prometheus metrics on the HOST (compose
# uses network_mode: host). These are set in docker-compose.yml (prometheus_port)
# and are NOT env-tunable here; scrape them locally, keep the ports firewalled:
#   livekit-server 127.0.0.1:6789 · ingress :6790 · egress :6791

# Brute-force rate limiting for the SENSITIVE auth endpoints only (login,
# magic-link, magic/verify). The rest of the API — incl.
# the dashboard's background polling — is NOT limited. Per client IP, per window.
#AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10           # max attempts per window (default 10)
#AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=900000 # window in ms (default 900000 = 15 min)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network security — in-app IP access control + auto-ban (fail2ban-style).
# Complements (never replaces) the reverse proxy / OS firewall. Loopback +
# private (RFC1918) IPs are ALWAYS permitted and NEVER banned, so you cannot
# lock yourself out. Rules/bans are managed at runtime from the dashboard
# (Settings -> Network security) or /api/v1/security/*.
# Docs: https://streamhub.studio/docs/features/network-security
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Allow/blocklist mode: off (default) | log (record, never reject) | enforce.
#STREAMHUB_IP_ACCESS_MODE=off
# Strict allowlist: in enforce mode, reject any PUBLIC IP without an allow rule.
#STREAMHUB_IP_ALLOWLIST_ONLY=false
# Auto-ban abusive IPs (failed logins / magic verifies / invalid tokens / 429s).
#STREAMHUB_AUTOBAN_ENABLED=false
#STREAMHUB_AUTOBAN_MAX_OFFENSES=10 # offenses within the window that trigger a ban
#STREAMHUB_AUTOBAN_WINDOW_S=300    # sliding offense window (seconds)
#STREAMHUB_AUTOBAN_BASE_TTL_S=900  # first ban duration; doubles per repeat (cap 7d)
#STREAMHUB_AUTOBAN_404_ENABLED=false # also count 404 storms as offenses

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Egress SSRF guard. Tenant-controlled outbound targets (per-app callback URL,
# custom S3 endpoint, MQTT broker URL, URL-pull ingress) are DENIED when they
# point at / resolve to private, loopback, link-local or metadata addresses
# (169.254.0.0/16, 127/8, 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, ::1, fc00::/7,
# fe80::/10, 0.0.0.0). The escapes below are operator-only, default OFF/empty.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated hostnames / IPs / CIDRs allowed as PRIVATE callback targets
# (e.g. an internal hooks service). Default empty = deny all private targets.
#STREAMHUB_CALLBACK_ALLOW_HOSTS=
# 1 = allow a private/loopback custom S3 endpoint (self-hosted MinIO on LAN).
#STREAMHUB_ALLOW_PRIVATE_S3_ENDPOINT=0
# 1 = allow a private/loopback MQTT broker URL (on-prem broker).
#STREAMHUB_ALLOW_PRIVATE_MQTT_BROKER=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups — daily versioned + gzip-compressed snapshots of EVERY SQLite DB
# (data/streamhub.db + each apps/<app>/app.db), taken with SQLite's safe
# hot-backup (VACUUM INTO — never a raw copy of a live DB) and stored under
# <DATA_DIR>/backups/<db>/<timestamp>.db.gz. Restore from the dashboard
# (Settings -> Database backups) or POST /api/v1/system/backups/restore.
# Docs: https://streamhub.studio/docs/operations/BACKUPS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled daily backups on/off (on-demand API backups always work).
#STREAMHUB_BACKUPS_ENABLED=true
# Prune snapshots older than N days after every run (0 = keep forever).
#STREAMHUB_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# Snapshot root override (default <DATA_DIR>/backups; use another disk/mount
# to survive the loss of the data volume).
#STREAMHUB_BACKUP_DIR=/data/backups
# Daily schedule (cron, server-local time). Invalid -> default 03:30.
#STREAMHUB_BACKUP_CRON=30 3 * * *

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resource-usage history (in-app, lightweight). Samples host CPU / RAM / Disk /
# Network ONCE PER HOUR into the global DB and renders dot charts on the
# dashboard's "Resource history" (superadmin) page. Complementary to the
# Prometheus/Grafana stack (that is the heavyweight path). Degrades gracefully
# on non-Linux hosts (Network needs Linux /proc; disk needs fs.statfsSync).
# API: GET /api/v1/system/metrics/history. Docs: operations/OBSERVABILITY.md §1b
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hourly sampler on/off (the read API still serves stored history when off).
#STREAMHUB_METRICS_HISTORY_ENABLED=true
# Prune samples older than N days after every run (0 = keep forever). 1h precision.
#STREAMHUB_METRICS_HISTORY_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# Sampling schedule (cron, server-local time). Invalid -> default hourly.
#STREAMHUB_METRICS_HISTORY_CRON=0 * * * *

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security audit trail (ISO 27002 §8.15/§8.16). Always on: security-relevant
# events (login success/failure, token mint/revoke, permission denials, config
# and S3/MQTT secret changes, db purges, IP-rule changes, moderation) are
# appended to the tamper-evident `audit_log` table (INSERT-only, hash-chained).
# Query it via GET /api/v1/system/audit (superadmin) or the dashboard's Audit
# page; live control posture at GET /api/v1/system/compliance.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Days audit entries are kept (purged ~90 s after boot and every 6 h).
# 0 = keep forever.
#STREAMHUB_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Shared secret that lets edge nodes register via POST /api/v1/cluster/join
# (install.sh --join sends it as X-Cluster-Token). Empty = joining disabled.
#STREAMHUB_CLUSTER_TOKEN=clt_change_me

# Redis URL handed to joining edges (defaults to redis://<--origin-ip>:6379 on
# the edge side if unset). Point it at a Redis reachable from the edges.
#STREAMHUB_CLUSTER_REDIS_URL=redis://10.0.0.1:6379

# Edge nodes only (written by install.sh --join): where livekit/ingress/egress
# find the CLUSTER redis (host:port [+ password]), and where LiveKit posts room
# webhooks (the ORIGIN's core — edges run no core). Origin/standalone: unset.
#LIVEKIT_REDIS_ADDRESS=10.0.0.1:6379
#LIVEKIT_REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me
#LIVEKIT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://media.example.com/api/v1/webhooks/livekit

# Egress affinity (join/common nodes) — installer only (--with-egress). DEFAULT
# off: a joined common node runs livekit+ingress but NOT egress, so recordings/HLS
# land on the ORIGIN's disk (else cross-node artifacts break ~50% until S3
# transport — architecture/CLUSTER-ROADMAP.md). Origin always runs egress.
#STREAMHUB_NODE_EGRESS=1

# Origin with --cluster-redis-bind (install.sh): extra redis bind + auth so
# edges can reach it. REDIS_BIND takes several IPs space-separated.
#REDIS_BIND=127.0.0.1 10.0.0.1
#REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resource limits (docker-compose, compose v2 non-swarm keys)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hard caps for the media services so one runaway session can't OOM the host.
# Defaults target an 8GB / 8-core box. EGRESS is the biggest risk: it drives a
# headless Chrome to composite rooms and its RAM grows with resolution/tracks.
# The installer auto-clamps EGRESS_CPUS to min(nproc, 4) on hosts with fewer
# than 4 vCPUs (room-composite egress needs >=4 to avoid a hard compose-up
# failure); an explicit value set here is always respected as-is.
#EGRESS_MEM_LIMIT=2g
#EGRESS_CPUS=4
#INGRESS_MEM_LIMIT=1g
#INGRESS_CPUS=1.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups (deploy/backup.sh — cron / systemd timer; see operations/BACKUPS.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host data dir to back up (streamhub.db + apps/<app>/app.db + secrets.json).
#BACKUP_DATA_DIR=/opt/streamhub/data
# Where local tarballs are kept (default <BACKUP_DATA_DIR>/backups).
#BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR=/opt/streamhub/data/backups
# Prune local + remote copies older than this many days.
#BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# S3 (or S3-compatible) target. Leave BACKUP_S3_BUCKET empty for local-only.
#BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=my-streamhub-backups
#BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.wasabisys.com   # omit for AWS S3
#BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=streamhub-backups
#BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
#BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=change-me
#BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=change-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Misc
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOG_LEVEL=info
NODE_ENV=production
